faq:
  - q: "How is an executive resume different from a regular resume?"
    a: "Three key differences: (1) The professional summary leads with organizational scope, not functional deliverables. 'Built and scaled a 60-person engineering organization' beats 'Led multiple engineering teams.' (2) Bullets describe decisions, strategy, and organizational impact — not tasks. (3) The resume signals leadership altitude: P&L ownership, board-level communication, cross-functional authority, and the scale of outcomes. A technical or functional resume optimized for an IC role will fail at the executive level because it does not tell the right story."
  - q: "How long should an executive resume be?"
    a: "Two pages — maximum. Some executives with very broad careers can justify 2.5 pages, but no more. Every line should earn its place. The test: if you removed this bullet, would the story be weaker? If not, cut it."
  - q: "What should an executive resume summary include?"
    a: "Five elements: (1) Your leadership level and scope: 'VP Engineering with 12 years of...' (2) The organizational scale you have operated at: team size, budget, revenue. (3) Your domain specialization: cloud-native platforms, enterprise fintech, B2B SaaS. (4) Your signature achievement or trajectory: scaled org from 15 to 80, IPO-through-exit, $0 to $200M ARR. (5) Targeting signal: 'now seeking a CTO or VP Engineering role at a Series B–D company.'"
  - q: "What are the most important sections of an executive resume?"
    a: "(1) Summary — sets the leadership altitude immediately. (2) Selected Achievements or Key Wins — 3–5 headline accomplishments in bullet or callout format, outside the timeline. (3) Experience — boards and decisions, not tasks. (4) Education and Board Roles — relevant for executive credibility."
  - q: "Should executives list all their early-career jobs?"
    a: "No. Executive resumes should go back 15–20 years at most. Roles older than 20 years can be listed in a single line as 'Earlier career: [brief summary].' The focus is the last 3–4 roles where executive scope is visible."
